  ],
  "aiContextParagraph": "The Indonesian House of Representatives recently approved the Asset Seizure Law, marking a significant development in the country's legal framework for criminal investigations. Following the legislation's passage, the government announced plans to form a dedicated institution tasked with managing and supervising assets confiscated through this new authority. While officials and observers anticipate that the law will create a more progressive approach to anti-corruption efforts, specific provisions have drawn scrutiny, including Article 72, which stipulates that the law cannot be applied retroactively to previous corruption cases. Additionally, discussions have emerged regarding the exclusion of illicit enrichment provisions from the final text. The Corruption Eradication Commission has indicated its readiness to implement the regulations as the government moves toward establishing the necessary institutional oversight bodies under the leadership of President Prabowo Subianto.",
